## Lesson 33 – Paging the Screen

The C64 only shows 24 rows at a time, yet our games take place in sprawling tunnels, decks, and cities. Paging the screen is how we trick the player into thinking the world extends beyond the monitor. We’ll build a simple navigator that lets you “walk” through a level by revealing it a few chunks at a time—your first taste of camera control.

> [📷 suggested: screenshots showing Page 1, Page 2, Page 3 of the navigator]

---

### The One-Minute Tour

- Store long-form descriptions in arrays, then reveal them a screenful at a time.
- Combine page indices with a fixed window size to simulate camera travel.
- Teach the player the controls directly on screen so they never feel lost.

---

### Example Program

```basic
NEW
10 REM *** SCREEN PAGER ***
20 LINES=6:TOTAL=18:DIM PAGE$(TOTAL-1)
30 FOR I=0 TO TOTAL-1:READ PAGE$(I):NEXT I
40 MAXPAGE=INT((TOTAL-1)/LINES)
50 SP=0:GOSUB 200
60 GET K$
70 IF K$="" THEN 60
80 IF K$="A" AND SP>0 THEN SP=SP-1:GOSUB 200
90 IF K$="D" AND SP<MAXPAGE THEN SP=SP+1:GOSUB 200
100 IF K$="Q" THEN END
110 GOTO 60
200 PRINT CHR$(147);"STARDOCK BRIEFING"
210 BASE=SP*LINES
220 FOR R=0 TO LINES-1
230 IDX=BASE+R
240 IF IDX>=TOTAL THEN PRINT"":GOTO 270
250 PRINT PAGE$(IDX)
270 NEXT R
280 PRINT:PRINT "A=PREV  D=NEXT  Q=QUIT"
290 PRINT "PAGE";SP+1;"OF";MAXPAGE+1
300 RETURN
310 DATA "AIRLOCK 01: PRESSURE STABLE"
320 DATA "AIRLOCK 02: CYCLE IN PROGRESS"
330 DATA "CARGO LIFT: STANDBY"
340 DATA "OBSERVATION DECK: ALL CLEAR"
350 DATA "MAINTENANCE: HUMMING QUIETLY"
360 DATA "CREW QUARTERS: LIGHTS DIMMED"
370 DATA "LAB 3: SPECIMEN CONTAINED"
380 DATA "LAB 4: DO NOT OPEN"
390 DATA "ENGINEERING: COOLANT NORMAL"
400 DATA "REACTOR: OUTPUT 84 PERCENT"
410 DATA "SHIELD ARRAY: CHARGING"
420 DATA "SENSORS: CONTACT LOST"
430 DATA "DRY DOCK: EMPTY"
440 DATA "DRONE BAY: ACTIVE"
450 DATA "HANGAR: ONE SHIP READY"
460 DATA "TURRETS: AUTO MODE"
470 DATA "DETENTION: OCCUPIED"
480 DATA "BRIDGE: AWAITS CAPTAIN"
```

Sample output (first page):

```
STARDOCK BRIEFING
AIRLOCK 01: PRESSURE STABLE
AIRLOCK 02: CYCLE IN PROGRESS
CARGO LIFT: STANDBY
OBSERVATION DECK: ALL CLEAR
MAINTENANCE: HUMMING QUIETLY
CREW QUARTERS: LIGHTS DIMMED

A=PREV  D=NEXT  Q=QUIT
PAGE 1 OF 3
```

Lines 20–40 set up a six-line viewing window over eighteen entries and calculate how many “pages” we can show. Lines 80–100 read keyboard input: `A` pages upward, `D` pages downward, `Q` exits. The subroutine at line 200 handles all drawing—clearing the screen, printing the correct slice of the array, and reminding the player of their controls.

> **Tip:** `CHR$(147)` clears the screen instantly. Use it before redrawing a fresh page so the player never sees junk from the last view.

---

### Experiment Section

- Change `LINES` to 4 or 8 to see how a tighter or wider window changes the feel.
- Add more `DATA` lines and watch the navigator scale automatically.
- Replace the text with short ASCII maps (`"####...."`) to page through floor plans instead of descriptions.
- Track the last visited page in a variable and restore it when the player comes back—perfect for menus.

> [🎥 suggested: short clip flipping through pages while the cursor highlights the on-screen prompt]

---

### Concept Expansion

Paging is a text-only version of a game camera. Instead of shoving everything into one crowded screen, you create a calm window and slide it over stored data. The same approach works for wider maps, dialogue trees, logs, or inventory lists. Next we’ll hook this window into `DATA`-driven levels so our world is more than a list of sentences.

---

### Game Integration

Imagine a space station exploration game. Each deck is stored as text rows, and the pager becomes your minimap browser:

```basic
CAMY=SP*LINES
PRINT "YOU ARE AT:";PAGE$(CAMY+CURSOR)
```

Tie `SP` to the player’s vertical position and the page viewer becomes a live scrolling “camera” that follows their progress without moving sprites yet.

